Author: Kent Gang <kent.y.gang@gmail.com>
Date:   Mon Sep 21 18:43:29 2026 +0300

    fix(workbench): an ask that failed is not an ask in flight, the keyboard is not dropped, and the panel check bites

    The independent review's must-fix list, and the should-fix list with it.

    **A readiness ask that failed looked exactly like one still in flight.**
    `App.tsx` collapsed both into `null`, so a `/api/readyz` that never
    answered left the first screen saying *asking the server whether this one
    can be run here* for ever — over an ask that had ended — while the strip
    below it printed the failure's own sentence. A screen asserting a question
    that is not being asked is the traceability veto, on the first screen a
    reviewer sees. The failure is passed down now, the cards read the shared
    vocabulary's own *the ask did not come back*, and the server's sentence is
    printed once under them. The launchpad's six states are enumerated in its
    docstring with the sentence each shows, and the failure path has a test at
    both levels.

    **The mouse route dropped the keyboard.** The way in unmounts the instant
    it is pressed, so a reader who tabbed to *Change this claim* and pressed
    Enter was left standing on nothing and the next Tab started again at the
    top of the page. The panel takes the keyboard when it opens — the reader's
    own act, and `graph/theKeyboard.ts`'s rule is about who decided — and the
    screen puts it back where it came from when the panel closes, or on the
    control that has replaced it where that is gone. The box takes it, not the
    first button, so nobody is one stray Space from an edit.

    **The geometric check could not fail.** The panel is a fixed 336 pixels,
    so nothing measured inside it varied with the window, and every section in
    it reads `overflow: visible`, so no control could land outside the
    content. It now scrolls each control into view, asserts it lies inside the
    part of the panel that is on the glass, and asks what is topmost at its
    own centre — which is the overlap test, the only one of the three that
    catches a sibling drawn over a button. Plus one assertion that does vary
    with the window: the page never scrolls sideways. The workbench chapter
    now says what is actually checked, and what still needs a person.

    **And the defect that check was written to find.** The three likelihood
    fields of *My own number* sat in `repeat(3, 1fr)` with no floor: an input
    with no `size` has a min-content width of about 140 pixels, so three of
    them wanted 430 inside a 303-pixel column and ran past the panel's edge.
    `minmax(0, 1fr)` and `min-width: 0`, which is the guard every other
    multi-track grid here already carries.

    **Four more dead declarations, with the same proof as the first three.**
    `.outline`'s `overflow-y`, and the `flex` and `min-height` on
    `.branch-panel`, `.intervene`, `.inspector` and `.delta-rail`: each of
    those five sections has exactly one render site, and every mount point of
    each is the `panel` slot of `MapFrame`, which is `.page--map .dock` — so
    `.page--map .dock > *` beats all of them by two classes to one. Checked
    per selector.

    Also: the vocabulary gains **Change this claim**, which was a label on
    screen and not in the Interface words table; the duplicate test the rebase
    left behind is gone; the sentences that name examples with no card name
    them in a few words rather than counting them; and the waiting card, which
    had a class and no rule, reads quieter than one that opens something.

    Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>
    Entire-Checkpoint: 01M32A5XKP1XPJMBSEVPDP2WW8

